    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n92967" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">σ᾽</span></b> , by apostr. for <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">δέ</span>;
		also, though rarely, for <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">σοί</span>; <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>A.</b>
				v.
				<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">σύ</span>
			. </div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense2"><b>II.</b> for <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">σά</span>, but in <i><span class="en">
				Hom.
			</span></i> only in phrases <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">τὰ σ᾽ αὐτοῦ, τὰ σ᾽
				αὐτῆς</span>, <i><span class="en">
				Od.
				14.185
			</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
				Il.
				6.490
			</span></i>, al.; so, in Trag. and later Poets, 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">τὰ σ᾽</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">
					S.
					<u>OT</u>
					329
				</span></i>
			,<i><span class="en">
				405
			</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
				<u>Ph.</u>
				339
			</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
				<u>El.</u>
				1499
			</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
				E.
				<u>Supp.</u>
				456
			</span></i>.</div>
